! A hydrogen atom in a box: the smallest magnetic system there is, and the one
! the noncollinear-equals-collinear identity is checked on.
!
! Why hydrogen and why norm-conserving. The identity "a noncollinear run with
! every moment along z reproduces the collinear LSDA run" is exact only where
! the density is non-negative: QE's nspin = 4 exchange-correlation energy uses
! |rho| as its prefactor (v_of_rho.f90, `arho = ABS(rho%of_r(ir,1))`) where the
! nspin = 1 and 2 branches use the signed density, so a cell whose truncated
! density rings negative in the vacuum makes the two conventions differ. This
! density is strictly positive on the whole grid -- a smooth one-electron
! charge from a norm-conserving dataset -- so the identity holds to round-off
! rather than to the size of that convention.
!
! The occupation is set by smearing rather than by fixed occupations because a
! noncollinear band holds one electron and a collinear one holds two, so
! 'fixed' would fill a different number of bands in the two runs.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 1, celldm(1) = 12.0,
    nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 25.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.02
    nspin = 2
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.6
    nbnd = 4
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 H  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 H 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS {automatic}
 1 1 1 0 0 0
